  "executive_briefing": "The late-2026 corporate tax landscape is characterized by the definitive operationalization of systemic tax frameworks and real-time indirect tax digitization. Multi-national tax planning is adjusting to the concrete implementation of the OECD's Pillar Two global minimum tax, which is now supported by comprehensive 2026 OECD Corporate Tax Statistics. Globally, statutory corporate income tax rates remain relatively stable, but effective rates are being compressed by targeted domestic revisions and targeted corporate rebates, such as Singapore's newly structured YA 2026 CIT rebates. Concurrently, indirect tax compliance has taken a structured leap forward, with jurisdictions like Singapore outlining definitive, phased timelines through 2031 for mandatory system-to-system Peppol e-invoicing. Meanwhile, international trade remains highly volatile, as unilateral tariffs and reciprocal non-tariff negotiations\u2014particularly between the US and China\u2014continue to force continuous restructuring of multinational supply chains.",
